<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guido Tarlati</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1327">1327</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guido Tarlati was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a> from 1312 and lord of the city from 1321 until his death in 1327, leading the Ghibelline party in Tuscany. He expanded Arezzo's territory, rebuilt its walls and crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Louis the Bavarian</a> King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1327, despite papal excommunication. His tomb in Arezzo cathedral, made by Agostino di Giovanni and Agnolo di Ventura, records his deeds.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guido-tarlati.jpg" alt="Guido Tarlati" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vincenzo Desiderio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CenotafioGuidoTarlatiparticolare.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Guido Tarlati belonged to the leading Ghibelline family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> lay at Pietramala. Two aristocratic factions, the Verdi and the Secchi, contended for control of the city, and when the Secchi gained the upper hand they had their leader, then Archpriest of the Pieve of S. Maria Aretina, elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. After the death of Bishop Ildebrandino the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> chose to proceed by the way of compromise, naming two canons of the cathedral to make the choice; they picked Guido and the others ratified it.</p><p>Procurators carried the record of the election to the papal court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>. Pope Clement V had three cardinals examine the election and the character of the candidate, and on their favourable report he confirmed Guido as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> on 7 July 1312.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> he built a wall around the city, issued a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coinage and waged wars of conquest: the names of Lusignano, Chiusi, Fronzoli, Castel Focognano, Rondina, Bucine, Caprese, Lacerina and Monte Sansovino appear on his funeral monument. In 1320 he commissioned the gilded three-storey altarpiece known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a> or Tarlati Polyptych for Santa Maria della Pieve, showing the Madonna and Child with Saints Donatus, John the Evangelist, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a> and Matthew.</p><p>As lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a> from 1321 he supported Uguccione della Faggiola and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castruccio-castracani">Castruccio Castracani</a>, lords of Lucca, in their wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, and in 1323, with Francesco I Ordelaffi, the Ghibelline lord of Forlì, he took Città di Castello. The growth of Arezzo soured relations with the Papal States. On 19 June 1325 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xxii">Pope John XXII</a> made Cortona, with its Guelph population, a diocese of its own, greatly reducing that of Arezzo, and Guido was excommunicated on 20 July 1325.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The pope placed the diocese under an apostolic administrator, Boso Ubertini, the Provost of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a>, who was named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> on 5 December 1326, but Tarlati kept him from carrying out his functions. His standing as leader in Tuscany of the Ghibelline party was such that the emperor Louis IV wished to receive the Iron Crown from him, and the coronation took place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> on 31 May 1327. A short time before his death Tarlati was reconciled with the pope.</p><p>His tomb in the Cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a> was commissioned by his brother, the condottiero Pier Saccone Tarlati di Pietramala. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giorgio-vasari">Giorgio Vasari</a> it was designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto</a>, who recommended the two Sienese sculptors who carried it out, though the attribution to Giotto is disputed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guido Tarlati” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its bishops remained political leaders: Guglielmino degli Ubertini died fighting Florence at Campaldino in 1289, and <b>Guido Tarlati</b>, bishop from 1312 and lord of the city from 1321 until his death in 1327, expanded the city's territory and rebuilt its walls.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-arezzo">Commune of Arezzo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.46,11.88&amp;year=1317" aria-label="Arezzo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4836 622 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Guido Tarlati"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5286 892h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5286 892h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.46,11.88&amp;year=1317">What was here in 1317? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
